# .reserved-terms: reserved-term list for the leak scanners.
#
# Copy this file to .reserved-terms (gitignored) and fill it with the
# project's internal-only strings: one extended-regex fragment per line.
# Blank lines and #-comments are ignored. The fragments are joined with
# "|" into a single alternation at scan time.
#
# The real list is deliberately NOT committed: publishing it would leak
# the very strings it exists to keep out of the public tree. Maintainers
# receive it out of band; CI sources it from the OW_RESERVED_TERMS
# repository secret.
#
# Example entries (fictional):
#
# internal-hostname\.example
# @private-domain\.example
# Secret Project Codename
# /home/(someuser)/
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Known limitation: fork PRs are green but UNSCANNED for reserved terms.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# GitHub never exposes repository secrets to a workflow run triggered by
# a pull request from a fork. OW_RESERVED_TERMS is therefore empty on
# those runs, and the reserved-term category is skipped by design: the
# alternative would be shipping the list to untrusted code, which is the
# exact leak this whole mechanism exists to prevent.
#
# The consequence has to be stated plainly, because it is easy to misread
# a green check as a clean tree:
#
#   A green Leak check on a FORK pull request is NOT evidence that the
#   tree is free of reserved terms. It only means the structural
#   categories passed. A maintainer MUST read a fork diff by eye before
#   merging it.
#
# The push-triggered run on dev/prod is the backstop, but it is a
# detection, not a prevention: by the time it fires, the term is already
# public. Eyes on fork diffs are the control that actually holds.
